1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaykaus_I '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kaykaus I''' or '''Kayka'us I''' or '''Keykavus I''' (Old Anatolian Turkish: كَیکاوس, Persian: عز الدين كيكاوس بن كيخسرو‎ ʿIzz ad-Dīn Kaykāwūs ibn Kaykhusraw) was the Sultan of Rum from 1211 until his death in 1220. He was the 
2 CONC eldest son of Kaykhusraw I.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Succession=
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the death of Kaykhusraw I following the Battle of Alaşehir in 1211,[1] Kaykaus’ two younger brothers, Kayferidun Ibrahim and the future Kayqubad I, challenged his succession. Kayqubad initially garnered some support among the
2 CONC  neighbors of the sultanate, Leo I, the king of Cilician Armenia, and Tughrilshah, his uncle and the independent ruler of Erzurum. At the same time, Kayferidun imperiled the recently acquired port of Antalya by seeking aid from th
2 CONC e Cypriot Franks. Most of the emirs, as the powerful landed aristocracy of the sultanate, supported Kaykaus. From his base in Malatya, Kaykaus seized Kayseri and then Konya, inducing Leo to change sides. Kayqubad was forced to fle
2 CONC e to the fortress at Ankara, where he sought aid from the Turkman tribes of Kastamonu. Kaykaus soon apprehended both of his brothers and secured the throne for himself.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During this time of considerable danger, Kaykaus negotiated a peace settlement with Theodore Laskaris, the Byzantine Emperor of Nicaea. This treaty marked the end of hostilities between the Seljuq state and the Empire of Nicaea, t
2 CONC hough Turkmen nomads continued occasionally to trouble the border.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT =The Eastern Frontier and the Fifth Crusade=
2 CONT
2 CONT With Antalya secure and the western marches at peace, Kaykaus turned his attentions to the east. During the Fifth Crusade, the crusaders allied with Kaykaus and forced the Ayyubids to undertake a two-front conflict.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Conquest of Sinop=
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaykaus’ most significant contribution to the Seljuq state was the acquisition the Black Sea port of Sinop. In 1214 Turkmen tribesmen captured Alexios, Grand Komnenos of the Empire of Trebizond, on a hunting trip outside of the ci
2 CONC ty. The hostage was turned over to the sultan and negotiated his freedom in exchange for Sinop and the vassalage of Trapezuntine territory to the east. The Seljuqs gained an outlet on the Black Sea to match their Mediterranean por
2 CONC t at Antalya, and a wedge was driven between the Empire of Trebizond and the Byzantine Empire of Nicaea. The transfer was affected on Sunday 1 November with both the sultan and the Grand Komnenos present. Alexios was entertained f
2 CONC or several days and then politely asked to return to Trebizond.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the transfer, European and Byzantine trade continued in the city. Kaykaus named an Armenian, Rais Hetoum, to govern the mixed Greek and Turkish population.[5] Between April and September 1215 the walls were reconstructed und
2 CONC er the supervision of the Greek architect, Sebastos. Fifteen Seljuq emirs contributed to the cost. The work is commemorated by a bilingual Greek and Arabic inscription on a tower near the western gate.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Monuments=
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217 Kaykaus built the so-called Şifaiye Medresesi in Sivas. The building was designed as a hospital and medical school. The sultan’s mausoleum is in the south eyvan of the building under a conical dome. The façade includes a p
2 CONC oem by the sultan in blue faience tiles.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Redford, Scott (1991). "The Alaeddin Mosque in Konya Reconsidered". Artibus Asiae (Zürich: Artibus Asiae Publishers) 51 (1/2): 54–74 [70]. doi:10.2307/3249676. ISSN 0004-3648. JSTOR 3249676.
2 CONT # Cahen, Claude (1968). Pre-Ottoman Turkey: A general survey of the material and spiritual culture and history c. 1071-1330. New York: Taplinger. pp. 120–121. ISBN 1-59740-456-X.
2 CONT # Cahen, Pre-Ottoman Turkey, p. 121
2 CONT # Anthony Bryer and Richard Winfield, The Byzantine Monuments and Topography of the Pontos, vol. 1, (Washington D.C.: Dumbarton Oaks, 1985) 71-72.
2 CONT # Cahen, Pre-Ottoman Turkey, p. 123
2 CONT # Bryer and Winfield, Pontos, 71-72.
2 CONT # Redford, Scott (1991). "The Alaeddin Mosque in Konya Reconsidered". Artibus Asiae (Zürich: Artibus Asiae Publishers) 51 (1/2): 54–74. ISSN 0004-3648. JSTOR 3249676.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alaeddin_Mosque '''Wikipedia'''] page of the Alaeddin Mosque in Konya:
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaykaus I began a major rebuilding program in 1219. He changed the main entrance from the west to the north, opposite the mihrab. He added a monumental façade on the north side, overlooking the city and facing the Seljuq palace. A
2 CONC  marble tomb was begun in the courtyard. Kaykaus’ building was cut short by his death in the same year, only to be resumed thereafter by his brother and successor Kayqubad I. Kayqubad had several of his brother's inscriptions alte
2 CONC red and claimed the improvements to the mosque for himself. In 1235 he added a large room, supported by forty-two columns, to the east of the mihrab.
