1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_of_Imereti '''Wikipedia'''], the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Michael''' (Georgian: მიქელი, Mik'el) (died 1329), from the House of Bagrationi, was king of the western Georgian kingdom of Imereti from 1327 to 1329.
2 CONT
2 CONT A son of the Georgian king David VI Narin and his wife, Tamar Amanelisdze,[1] or a Palaeologian princess. In the latter case, Michael might have been named after his Byzantine ancestor, the emperor Michael VIII Palaiologos.[2] Mic
2 CONC hael opposed accession of his elder brother, Constantine I, on the death of their father in 1293. In a subsequent internecine war, Michael seized control of the provinces of Racha, Lechkhumi, and Argveti.[3][1][1] The conflict con
2 CONC tinued until 1327, when Michael succeeded on the death of the childless Constantine as king of Imereti,[3][1] although he had claimed the title earlier, as in the 1326 charter[2] sanctioning a reparational payment (sasiskhlo, a Ge
2 CONC orgian equivalent of weregild) by a certain Gogitashvili to Mikeladze.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael sought to resubjugate to the crown the great nobles and provincial dynasts (eristavi), who had asserted greater autonomy for themselves in the reign of Constantine I. His efforts were of limited success; all he could achie
2 CONC ve was the pledge from the eristavi to pay tribute and provide troops for a royal army.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael died in 1329. He was succeeded by his son, Bagrat I, who, owing to his minority, never firmly sat on the throne of Imereti and was reduced to a vassal duke by the resurgent eastern Georgia, George V "the Brilliant", in 133
2 CONC 0.[3][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT =References=
2 CONT
2 CONT # Rayfield, Donald (2012). Edge of Empires: A History of Georgia. London: Reaktion Books. pp. 132, 139–140. ISBN 1780230303.
2 CONT # Toumanoff, Cyril (1949–51). "The Fifteenth-Century Bagratids and the Institution of Collegial Sovereignty in Georgia". Traditio 7: 173.
2 CONT # Bagrationi, Vakhushti (1976). Nakashidze, N.T., ed. История Царства Грузинского [History of the Kingdom of Georgia] (PDF) (in Russian). Tbilisi: Metsniereba. p. 41.
2 CONT # Taqaishvili, Ekvtime (1909). საქართველოს სიძველენი, ტომი II [Antiquities of Georgia, Vol. 2] (PDF) (in Georgian). Tiflis: Georgian Society of History and Ethnography. pp. 33–34.
