1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * from [https://books.google.com/books?id=9CYiDAAAQBAJ&pg=PA187&lpg=PA187&dq=Constantine+Melek&source=bl&ots=aLnrmThdTn&sig=vmQ8Mk8CRTwZ9P8VjM_B-3NTZJk&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwimmcfIzJjNAhWBMSYKHVnJBcYQ6AEIIDAA#v=one
2 CONC page&q=Constantine%20Melek&f=false '''The Byzantine Turks, 1204-1461'''], by Rustam Shukurov.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Melik/Melikes/Melek Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Another popular name for nobles and prominent persons was Melik (variant spellings Melek and Melikes). In fact, there did exist the noble family of the Meliks, founded by ''sebastokrator'' or ''caesar'' Konstantinos Melek (d. afte
2 CONC r 1306), the son of the sultan ‘Izz al-Din Kaykawus. Constantine Melek was the governor of Pegai on behalf of Andronikos II and later, probably, became the governor of Berroia...
2 CONT
2 CONT ... Constantine Melek was a first-generation immigrant, most like born in Seljuk Anatolia, and by 1264/65 at the time of his father’s flight from Byzantium, was probably underage and stayed with the sultan’s women. 
