1 NAME Komnene /of Trebizond/
2 GIVN Komnene
2 _MARNM of Trebizond
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Komnene, daughter of Alexios I of Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Komnene was the wife of Andronikos I of Trebizond. Her first name is unknown. Komnene is the female form of "Komnenos", her family name [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the only known daughter of Alexios I of Trebizond and Theodora Axuchina. Her siblings included John I of Trebizond and Manuel I of Trebizond. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Manuel Komnenos and of Rusudan. Her maternal grandfather was probably John Komnenos Axuch. John was a short-lived rival emperor to Alexios III Angelos. On 31 July 1200, John was proclaimed Emperor in
2 CONC  Hagia Sophia. He was betrayed and killed by his own soldiers, defecting back to the service of Alexios. [3] "A Genealogy of the Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond" by Kelsey Jackson Williams, gives his nickname as "John the Fat". [4] Wi
2 CONC lliams theorises that Komnene's maternal grandmother was a daughter of John II Komnenos and Piroska of Hungary. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT Komnene married Andronikos Gidos. The chronicle of Michael Panaretos gives his name as "Gidon". The origin of this family name is unknown. The article "Byzantine Trebizond:A Provincial Literary Landscape" by Jan Olof Rosenqvist su
2 CONC ggests that it may originate in the first name Guido, indicating Italian origins. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT An Andronikos Gidos is recorded by Niketas Choniates to have served under Theodore I Laskaris, Emperor of Nicaea. In 1206, this Andronikos ambushed a force of three hundred troops of the Latin Empire at Tracheiai, near Nicomedia. 
2 CONC The invading force had been lend by Henry of Flanders to David Komnenos as part of their alliance against Theodore I. [7] Whether this Gidos was her husband or a namesake relative of his is uncertain.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 February 1222, Alexios I died. He was succeeded not by his sons but by his son-in-law, Andronikos. Gidos was probably already a prominent member of the military, while his brothers-in-law were still underage. [9] Komnene was 
2 CONC his Empress consort, succeeding her mother. Their marriage seems to have been childless. Andronikos died in 1235, succeeded by her brother John I of Trebizond. [10]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Komnene was the wife of Andronikos I of Trebizond. Her first name is unknown. Komnene is the female form of "Komnenos", her family name [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the only known daughter of Alexios I of Trebizond and Theodora Axuchina. Her siblings included John I of Trebizond and Manuel I of Trebizond. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Manuel Komnenos and of Rusudan. Her maternal grandfather was probably John Komnenos Axuch. John was a short-lived rival emperor to Alexios III Angelos. On 31 July 1200, John was proclaimed Emperor in
2 CONC  Hagia Sophia. He was betrayed and killed by his own soldiers, defecting back to the service of Alexios. [3] "A Genealogy of the Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond" by Kelsey Jackson Williams, gives his nickname as "John the Fat". [4] Wi
2 CONC lliams theorises that Komnene's maternal grandmother was a daughter of John II Komnenos and Piroska of Hungary. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Empress
2 CONT
2 CONT Komnene married Andronikos Gidos. The chronicle of Michael Panaretos gives his name as "Gidon". The origin of this family name is unknown. The article "Byzantine Trebizond:A Provincial Literary Landscape" by Jan Olof Rosenqvist su
2 CONC ggests that it may originate in the first name Guido, indicating Italian origins. [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT An Andronikos Gidos is recorded by Niketas Choniates to have served under Theodore I Laskaris, Emperor of Nicaea. In 1206, this Andronikos ambushed a force of three hundred troops of the Latin Empire at Tracheiai, near Nicomedia. 
2 CONC The invading force had been lend by Henry of Flanders to David Komnenos as part of their alliance against Theodore I. [7] Whether this Gidos was her husband or a namesake relative of his is uncertain.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 February 1222, Alexios I died. He was succeeded not by his sons but by his son-in-law, Andronikos. Gidos was probably already a prominent member of the military, while his brothers-in-law were still underage. [8] Komnene was 
2 CONC his Empress consort, succeeding her mother. Their marriage seems to have been childless. Andronikos died in 1235, succeeded by her brother John I of Trebizond. [2]
2 CONT
