1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Lady Anna of Lampron, known as Keran of Lampron''' (before 1262 – 28 July 1285) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Born '''''Anna''''', she was called''' ''Kir-Anna''''' (meaning Lady Anna) after her husband took the throne, which was later shortened to '''''Keran''''', or sometimes written ''Guerane'', and after the birth of her last son Kera
2 CONC n became a nun and entered the Monastery of Trazarg assuming the name of '''''Theophania'''''.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a by-birth member of the House of Lampron and by marriage Queen-Consort of Armenia.  She was the daughter of Prince Hethum of Lampron by his unknown wife, who probably was from Frankish origin. She had three known siblings
2 CONC : Marianne, Alix (later wife of Balian d'Ibelin, Seneschal of Cyprus), and Raymond Lord of Michael'gla.  Before 15 January 1262/14, Keran married Prince Leo of Armenia (eldest son and heir of King Hethum I) who became King after h
2 CONC is father's abdication in 1270.   Many words of praise were made about Queen Keran by her contemporaries. Her son Hethum claimed that ''"she had a wonderful soul and a beautiful body."'' The chronicler and scribe Avetis described 
2 CONC her as ''"a good friend to her husband in trouble and joy."'' She died on 28 July 1285 and was buried in the Monastery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keran,_Queen_of_Armenia
2 CONT * https://dbpedia.org/page/Keran,_Queen_of_Armenia
2 CONT * https://www.biographies.net/biography/keran,-queen-of-armenia/m/08_btg
2 CONT * https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Keran,_Queen_of_Armenia
2 CONT * https://everything.explained.today/Keran,_Queen_of_Armenia/
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/232651109/keran_(guerane)-of_cilicia
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth date approx. Keran (also known as Guerane) of Lampron Castle, Armenia Kingdom of Lampron, was a by-birth a member of the House of Lampron and by marriage Queen Consort of the Armenia Kingdom of Cilicia. She was the daughter 
2 CONC of Prince Hethum of Lampron Castle.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT After the birth of her last son, Keran became a nun and entered the Monastery of Trazarg (now called Drazark Monastery), assuming the name of Theophania. She was probably joined there by her sister Marianne. She died on 28 July 12
2 CONC 85 and was buried in the Monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [Source of the Illustration of Their five Children in 1272 :
2 CONT
2 CONT Armenian manuscript, from "Le Royaume Armenien de Cilicie", photographed by Claude Mutafian "The Gospel [of Queen Keran] was commissioned by Queen Keran for her husband and future king Levon of the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia. It 
2 CONC was copied by the scribe Avetis in the capital city of Sis in 1272 and illustrated by Roslin. The manuscript was bound in Jerusalem in gold repousse work by the monks of the St James Brotherhood. The front cover represents the Cru
2 CONC cifixion, and the back the Virgin and Child; on the claps are the four Evangelists." Includes the only two children born before 1272 who lived to adulthood, though were then murdered - Hethum II and Thoros III.]
