1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_II,_King_of_Armenia
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo II or Leon II (occasionally numbered Leo III; Armenian: Լեւոն Բ, Levon II; c. 1236–1289) was king of the Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia, ruling from 1269/1270 to 1289. He was the son of King Hetoum I and Queen Isabella and was a 
2 CONC member of the Hetoumid family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo was born in 1236, the son of King Hetoum I and Queen Isabella. Hetoum and Isabella's marriage in 1226 had been a forced one by Hetoum's father Constantine of Baberon, who had arranged for Queen Isabella's first husband to be m
2 CONC urdered so as to put Constantine's own son Hetoum in place as a co-ruler with Isabella. They had six children, of which Leo was the eldest. One of his sisters was Sibylla of Armenia, who was married to Bohemond VI of Antioch to br
2 CONC ing peace between Armenia and Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1262 Leo married Keran (Kir Anna), the daughter of Prince Hetoum of Lampron.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1266, while their father king Hetoum I was away to visit the Mongol court, Leo and his younger brother Thoros fought to repel Mamluk invaders, at the Battle of Mari. Thoros was killed in combat, and Leo, along with 40,000 other
2 CONC  Armenian soldiers was captured and imprisoned. When King Hetoum returned, he paid a large ransom to retrieve his son, including a large quantity of money, handing over several fortresses, and accepting to intercede with the Mongo
2 CONC l ruler Abagha in order to have one of Baibars's relatives freed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Hetoum I abdicated in 1269 in favour of his son, and entered the Franciscan order. He died a year later. The new king Leo II was known as a pious king, devoted to Christianity. He pursued active commercial relations with the West,
2 CONC  by renewing trade agreements with the Italians and establishing new ones with the Catalans. He also endeavoured to reinforce the Mongol alliance as his father Hetoum I had submitted Armenia to Mongol authority in 1247.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1271, Marco Polo visited the Armenian harbour of Ayas and commented favourably about Leo's reign and the abundance of the country, although he mentions his military forces were rather demoralized:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The king [Leo II] properly maintains justice in his land, and is a vassal of the Tartars. There are many cities and villages, and everything in abundance.(...) In the past, men were courageous at war, but today they are vile and 
2 CONC chetive, and don't have other talents than drink properly."
2 CONT
2 CONT —Marco Polo "Le Livre des Merveilles"
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1275 the Mamluk sultan Baibars invaded Cilicia for a second time. The following year, Armenia fought off an invasion by the Turkomans, but the Constable Sempad, Leo's uncle, was killed in combat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mongol alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Armeno-Mongol alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1281 Leo joined the Mongols in their invasion of Syria, but they were vanquished at the Second Battle of Homs. Leo had to sue for peace, and in 1285 obtained a 10-year truce in exchange for important territorial concessions in 
2 CONC favour of the Mamluks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leo died in 1289, and was succeeded by his son Hetoum II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendance
2 CONT
2 CONT During twenty-one years of marriage Leo had sixteen children by his wife Keran, ten sons and six daughters. Three sons and three daughters died at an early age. Five of his children reached the throne. The eldest, Hetoum II of Arm
2 CONC enia, abdicated after four years in favor of his younger brother Thoros III of Armenia, but was placed back on the throne in 1294. In 1296, their brother Sempad of Armenia strangled Thoros and blinded Hetoum, in order to seize pow
2 CONC er. Sempad was then overthrown in 1298 by their younger brother Constantine III of Armenia, who was replaced by older brother Hetoum, who then abdicated in 1305 in favor of Thoros's son Leo III of Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1.Son (b. 15 January 1262/14 January 1263 – d. young).
2 CONT
2 CONT  2.Constantine (b. June 1265 – d. young).
2 CONT
2 CONT  3.Fimi [Euphemia] (b. 14 January 1266/13 January 1267 – d. young).
2 CONT
2 CONT  4.Hethum II (b. 14 January 1266/13 January 1267 – murdered 7 November 1307), King of Armenia (ruled 1289 to 1293, 1294 to 1297, 1299 to 1307).
2 CONT
2 CONT  5.Isabella [Zabel] (b. 13 January 1269/12 January 1270 – d. bef. 1273).
2 CONT
2 CONT  6.Thoros III (b. October 1270 – murdered 23 July 1298), King of Armenia (ruled 1293 to 1298).
2 CONT
2 CONT  7.Ruben (b. 13 January 1272/12 January 1273 – d. young)
2 CONT
2 CONT  8.Isabella [Zabel] (b. 12 January 1273/11 January 1274 – d. bef. 1276).
2 CONT
2 CONT  9.Sempad (b. 12 January 1276/11 January 1277 – d. 1310 or 1311), King of Armenia (ruled 1297 to 1299).
2 CONT
2 CONT  10.Isabella (b. 12 January 1276/11 January 1277 – murdered May 1323), twin with Sempad; married in 1293 with Amalric of Lusignan, Prince of Tyre, son of King Hugh III of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT  11.Constantine I (b. 11 January 1277/10 January 1278 – d. aft. 1308), King of Armenia (ruled 1299).
2 CONT
2 CONT  12.Rita (b. 11 January 1278/10 January 1279 – July 1333), renamed Maria upon her wedding; married in 1294 with Michael IX Palaeologus, co-Emperor of the Byzantine Empire with his father Andronicus II Palaeologus.
2 CONT
2 CONT  13.Theophanu (b. 11 January 1278/10 January 1279 – d. 1296), renamed Teodora upon her betrothal; she died in route to married Theodore, son of John I Doukas, Lord of Thessaly.
2 CONT
2 CONT  14.Nerses (b. 11 January 1279/10 Jan 1280 – d. 26 May 1301), a priest.
2 CONT
2 CONT  15.Oshin (b. 10 January 1283/9 January 1284 – murdered 20 July 1320), King of Armenia (ruled 1308 to 1320).
2 CONT
2 CONT  16.Alinakh (b. 10 January 1283/9 January 1284] – d. 28 August 1310), twin with Oshin; Lord of Lampron and Tarsus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the sixteen children, only ten made it to adulthood and five of them, Hethum, Thoros, Sempad, Constantine and Oshin, would later become Armenian Kings. They often fought each other to keep or gain the throne, but it were the de
2 CONC scendants of their sister Isabella that finally inherited the throne.
2 CONT
