1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Kadi Burhan al-Din==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Qāḍī Aḥmad Burhān al-Dīn''' (Turkish: Kadı Burhâneddin; Azerbaijani: Qazi Bürhanəddin; 8 January 1345 – 1398) was vizier to the Eretnid rulers of Anatolia. In 1381, he took over Eretnid lands and claimed the title of sultan for
2 CONC  himself. He is most often referred to by the title Qadi, a name for Islamic judges, which was his first occupation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadi_Burhan_al-Din En]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadi_Burhan_al-Din#Early_life_and_education Early life and education]===
2 CONT
2 CONT Aḥmad was born on 8 January 1345 in Kayseri in central Anatolia, the capital of the Eretnid Sultanate. Aḥmad's father was Shams al-Dīn Muḥammad, who like his father, Sirāj al-Dīn,[1] and grandfather, was a qāḍī (judge). Aḥmad's pa
2 CONC ternal lineage belonged to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salur_(tribe)_ Sālūr], and [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oghuz_Turks Oghuz] tribe originally inhabiting the region of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khwarazm Khwarazm] in 
2 CONC [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Asia Central Asia].[2] This tribe had earlier migrated to [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kastamonu Kastamonu] before arriving in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kayseri Kayseri]. A granddaught
2 CONC er of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seljuk_dynasty Seljuk] [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Rum Sultan of Rum], [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaykhusraw_II Kaykhusraw II], Aḥmad's mother was the daughter of Abdull
2 CONC ah Chelebī, son of Jalal al-Dīn Maḥmud Mustavfī, who was an influential figure during the Seljuk rule in Anatolia.[3] Following the political struggle caused by [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eretna Eretna's] death, Aḥmad and his 
2 CONC father spent 4 months in exile in Syria in 1356.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aḥmad initially received his education from his father Muḥammad and later studied in Egypt, Damascus, and Aleppo.[5] He mastered Arabic, Persian, and the Islamic sciences, and undertook the Hajj. He further assisted his father in 
2 CONC his work as the judge of Kayseri.[4] When he returned to his hometown in 1364–5, one year after his father's death, the sultan, [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghiyath_al-Din_Muhammad_I Ghiyāth al-Dīn Muhammad I], esteemed his educ
2 CONC ation and character, granting Aḥmad the title qāḍī but also marrying him to his daughter.[5] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadi_Burhan_al-Din#Rise_to_power Rise to power]===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadi_Burhan_al-Din#Reign Reign]===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kadi_Burhan_al-Din#Poetry Poetry]===
2 CONT
