2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_IV_of_Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT Two further daughters have been attributed to Alexios by later genealogists. Michel Kuršanskis has argued these marriages never really happened, and their existence is based on a misreading of an interpolation to Chalcondyles.[16]
2 CONC  These daughters are:
2 CONT
2 CONT *Theodora of Trebizond, presumed wife of Ali Beg of the Ak Koyunlu. There is not mention of this marriage in any contemporany source. It is possible that she was confused with Theodora Despina, daughter of John IV and wife of Uzun
2 CONC  Hassan, son of Ali Beg. An other option was that the Alexios' Theodora married instead Osman Beg, father of Ali.
2 CONT *Eudoksia Valenza of Trebizond, wife of Niccolò Crispo, Lord of Syros. She was proposed as the daughter of Alexios when Ambassador Caterino Zeno's claim in his notes that she was the daughter of John IV proved to be false or an hi
2 CONC s error. Other historians argue instead that she was a Genoese woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Williams, Kelsey Jackson.  ''A Genealogy of the Grand Komnenoi of Trebizond'' (1/2007)< [https://research-repository.st-andrews.ac.uk/handle/10023/8570 link] > Page 183.  PDF page 13.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios IV, Emperor of Trebizond, 1417-1429 … 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kuršankis (1979, p.246) summarizes by stating that he believes Alexios to have had five children: Ioannes, Alexander, David, Maria and the anonymous wife of Qara Yoluq. He doubts the existence of the other three daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue by Theodora Kantakouzene (Nicol, 1968, pp.169-170)
2 CONT
2 CONT vii. (possibly) Anonyma III. Numerous secondary sources claim that ‘Alī ibn Qara ‘Uthmān, Jalāl al-Dīn, Emir of the Āq Quyūnlū (r.1435-1438) and son of Qara ‘Uthmān, married a daughter of Alexios IV but Bryer’s investigation of th
2 CONC is marriage (1975b, p.149) revealed only an obscure and doubtful passage in Chalkokondyles (Bekker, 1843, p.462) as its source.
