1 NOTE {geni:about_me} {{https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e0/Signature_of_Khass_Murad.png|right|border|105x50px|Official signature (pençe) of Hass Murad Pasha, from a 1471/72 deed confirming a land grant in Bosnia|}}'''Ha
2 CONC ss Murad Pasha'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Hass Murad Pasha was an Ottoman statesman and commander of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_Greeks Byzantine Greek] origin. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hass_Murad_Pasha En], [https://tr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Has_Murad_Pa%C5%9Fa Tr]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hass_Murad_Pasha#Origin Origin]===
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 16th-century Ecthesis Chronica, [https://www.geni.com/people/Hass-Murad-Pasha/6000000205092642826 '''Hass Murad'''] and his brother, [https://www.geni.com/people/Mesih-Pasha/6000000205090503896 '''Mesih Pasha'''],
2 CONC  were sons of a certain [https://www.geni.com/people/Gidos-Palaiologos/6000000205092417878 '''Gidos Palaiologos'''], identified by the contemporary Historia Turchesca as a brother of a Byzantine Emperor.[2][3] This is commonly hel
2 CONC d to have been [https://www.geni.com/people/Constantine-XI-Palaiologos-last-byzantine-emperor/6000000009965401953 '''Constantine XI Palaiologos'''], the last Byzantine emperor, who fell during the fall of Constantinople to the Ott
2 CONC oman Sultan [https://www.geni.com/people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II'''] in 1453. If true, since [https://www.geni.com/people/Constantine-XI-Palaiologos-last-byzantine-emper
2 CONC or/6000000009965401953 '''Constantine XI'''] died childless, and if the Ottomans had failed to conquer Constantinople, [https://www.geni.com/people/Mesih-Pasha/6000000205090503896 '''Mesih'''] or [https://www.geni.com/people/Hass-
2 CONC Murad-Pasha/6000000205092642826 '''Hass Murad'''] might have succeeded him.[4] The brothers were captured during the fall of Constantinople, converted to Islam, and raised as pages under the auspices of Sultan [https://www.geni.co
2 CONC m/people/Mehmed-II-bin-Murad-Han-The-Conqueror-Ottoman-Sultan/385223927880013853 '''Mehmed II'''] as part of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devshirme devşirme] system.[2][5] The exact identity of his father is unclear; [https:
2 CONC //en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Sphrantzes Sphrantzes ] adds the name of "Thomas" to "Gidos", while several scholars, beginning with [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Crusius Martin Crusius], rather improbably equated the latte
2 CONC r name to the Venetian "Guido", Latin "Vitus".[6] However, neither Murad nor his brother can be identified with any of the known sons of Constantine XI's brothers who survived into adulthood — [https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-P
2 CONC alaiologos/6000000000670173610 '''Thomas'''], [https://www.geni.com/people/Prince-Demetrios-Palaiologos-Despot-of-Mistra/6000000008415722429 '''Demetrios'''], and [https://www.geni.com/people/Theodore-II-Palaiologos-despot-of-More
2 CONC a/361248587300001388 '''Theodore'''] — leading [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franz_Babinger Franz Babinger] to suggest an illegitimate origin.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hass_Murad_Pasha#Career_and_death Career and death]===
2 CONT When [https://www.geni.com/people/Mahmud-Pasha-Angelovi%C4%87/6000000205117325825 '''Mahmud Pasha Angelović'''] was dismissed as Grand Vizier and beylerbey (governor-general) of the Rumelia Eyalet in c. 1468, Hass Murad succeeded 
2 CONC him in the latter position. He seems to have held it until 1473, when he participated in a campaign against the Ak Koyunlu ruler Uzun Hassan in Anatolia, in which he was defeated and killed.[11][12] During this campaign, he comman
2 CONC ded the army's right wing a force of 20,000 men with 40 sanjakbeys.[13] Uzun Hassan retreated before the Ottomans beyond the Euphrates. According to some Ottoman historians, Mahmud Pasha, who served under Murad's command, tried to
2 CONC  warn him that the Ak Koyunlu were known to employ feigned retreats and that he should follow them cautiously, but Murad, who is portrayed as young, impetuous, and eager to claim glory for himself, did not heed him. Instead he mov
2 CONC ed ahead with part of his army, crossed the Euphrates, and was caught in an ambush on 4 August. The ensuing Battle of Tercan was a disaster for the Ottomans: Murad was killed—although Uzun Hassan's letter to the Doge of Venice cla
2 CONC ims that he was captured—along with a large part of the Ottoman army, while several distinguished commanders, including Turahanoğlu Ömer Bey and Fenarioğlu Ahmed Pasha, were taken prisoner. Other historians, however, including con
2 CONC temporary Greek and Latin sources, accuse Mahmud Pasha of knowing about the ambush and failing to warn Murad, because he was jealous of him.[14][15]
