1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/carlo-i-duca-di-savoia_(Dizionario-Biografico)/
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_I,_Duke_of_Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles I''' (29 March 1468 Carignano – 13 March 1490 Pinerolo), surnamed '''the Warrior''', was the Duke of Savoy from 1482 to 1490 and titular king of Cyprus, Jerusalem, and Armenia from 1485 to 1490.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was related in two ways to the childless Queen Charlotte of Cyprus, titular Queen of Armenia and Jerusalem. Not only was she, his father Amadeus' first cousin, in such a way that her rights would naturally descent to this 
2 CONC line, but also she was the widow of Charles' paternal uncle Louis of Savoy, Count of Geneva (d. 1482). Charlotte however was without a kingdom, having been exiled in the 1460's from her own legitimate kingdom of Cyrus by her illeg
2 CONC itimate half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT This double relationship would serve Charles well, as in 1485, when he was 17, she surrendered her rights in Cyprus, Armenia and Jerusalem to young Charles, her next legitimate heir in line from King Janus of Cyprus and Armenia. T
2 CONC he kingdom itself was held by the republic of Venice, but the Savoy dynasty continued to claim it.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Blanche of Montferrat (Bianca di Montferrato) (1472–1519) who after Charles's death was the regent of the Duchy of Savoy from 1490 to 1496. They had two surviving children.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Yolande Luise (1487–1499), married Philibert II of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT #Charles John Amadeus of Savoy (1489–1496).
2 CONT
