2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy (September 11, 1476 – September 22, 1531) was the mother of Francis I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise was born at Point d'Ain, the eldest daughter of Philip II, Duke of Savoy (1443–1497) and his first wife, Margaret of Bourbon (1438–1483). Her brother, Philibert II, Duke of Savoy (1480–1504), succeeded her father as ruler o
2 CONC f the duchy and head of the House of Savoy. He was, in turn, succeeded by their half-brother Charles III, Duke of Savoy (1486–1553).
2 CONT
2 CONT At age twelve, Louise married Charles de Valois (1459–1496), Count of Angoulême, on February 16, 1488 in Paris. This was Charles' third marriage. Their first child, Marguerite, was born on April 11, 1492; their second child, Franç
2 CONC ois, was born on September 12, 1494.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise had a keen awareness for the intricacies of politics and diplomacy, and was deeply aware of the advances of arts and sciences in Renaissance Italy. She made certain that her children were educated in the spirit of the Itali
2 CONC an Renaissance, also helped by her Italian confessor, Cristoforo Numai from Forlì. When she was widowed at the young age of 19, Louise deftly maneuvered her children into a position that would secure for each of them a promising f
2 CONC uture. She moved her family to the court of King Louis XII, her husband's cousin. Francis became a favorite of the king, who gave him his daughter Claude de France in marriage on 8 May 1514. With that marriage, Louis XII designate
2 CONC d Francis as his heir. With the death of Louis XII on 1 January 1515, Francis became king of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 4, 1515, Louise was named Duchess of Angoulême, and on April 15, 1524, Duchess of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother having been one of the sisters of the last dukes of the main branch of Bourbon, after the death of Susan, Duchess of Bourbon in 1521, she, on basis of proximity of blood, advanced claims to the Duchy of Auvergne and oth
2 CONC er possessions of the Bourbons. This lead her (supported by her son the king) in rivalry against Charles III, Duke of Bourbon, Susan's widower, whom she proposed to marry in order to settle the Bourbon inheritance issue. When reje
2 CONC cted by Charles, Louise instigated efforts to undermine him. This led to Charles' exile and his attempt to regain his lost status by waging war against the King. He died in 1527 having failed to regain his lost lands and titles. L
2 CONC ouise recovered Auvergne from confiscations and became duchess.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy remained active on behalf of her son in the early years of his reign especially. During his absences, she acted as regent on his behalf. She was the principal negotiator for the Treaty of Cambrai between France and
2 CONC  the Holy Roman Empire, concluded on August 3, 1529. That treaty, called "the Ladies Peace", put an end to the second Italian war between the head of the Valois dynasty, Francis I of France, and the head of the Habsburg dynasty, C
2 CONC harles V, Holy Roman Emperor. The Treaty temporarily confirmed Habsburg hegemony in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The treaty was signed by Louise of Savoy for France and her sister-in-law, Margaretha von Habsburg (Margaret of Austria), for the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy died on September 22, 1531, in Gretz-sur-Loing. The story of her death is that she became chilled while watching a comet.[1]Her remains were entombed at Saint-Denis in Paris. After her death her lands, including Au
2 CONC vergne, merged in the crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy (September 11, 1476 – September 22, 1531) was the mother of Francis I of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy was born at Pont-d'Ain, the eldest daughter of Philip II, Duke of Savoy (1443–1497) and his first wife, Margaret of Bourbon (1438–1483). Her brother, Philibert II, Duke of Savoy (1480–1504), succeeded her father as
2 CONC  ruler of the duchy and head of the House of Savoy. He was, in turn, succeeded by their half-brother Charles III, Duke of Savoy (1486–1553).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT At age eleven, Louise married Charles of Orléans (1459–1496), Count of Angoulême, on 16 February 1488 in Paris. Their first child, Marguerite, was born on 11 April 1492; their second child, François, was born on 12 September 1494.
2 CONT
2 CONT The household of Charles was presided over by his chatelaine Antoinette de Polignac, Dame de Combronde, by whom he had two illegitimate daughters, Jeanne of Angouleme and Madeleine. Antoinette became Louise's lady-in-waiting and c
2 CONC onfidante. Her children were raised alongside Louise's own.[1] Charles had another illegitimate daughter, Souveraine, by Jeanne le Conte, who also lived in the Angouleme chateau.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise had a keen awareness for the intricacies of politics and diplomacy, and was deeply interested in the advances of arts and sciences in Renaissance Italy. She made certain that her children were educated in the spirit of the 
2 CONC Italian Renaissance, also helped by her Italian confessor, Cristoforo Numai from Forlì. When she was widowed at the young age of 19, Louise deftly maneuvered her children into a position that would secure for each of them a promis
2 CONC ing future. She moved her family to the court of King Louis XII, her husband's cousin. Francis became a favorite of the king, who gave him his daughter Claude de France in marriage on 8 May 1514. With that marriage, Louis XII desi
2 CONC gnated Francis as his heir. With the death of Louis XII on 1 January 1515, Francis became king of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 February 1515, Louise was named Duchess of Angoulême, and on 15 April 1524, Duchess of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Inheritance
2 CONT
2 CONT Her mother having been one of the sisters of the last dukes of the main branch of Bourbon, after the death of Suzanne, Duchess of Bourbon in 1521, Louise, on basis of proximity of blood, advanced claims to the Duchy of Auvergne an
2 CONC d other possessions of the Bourbons. This led her (supported by her son the king) in rivalry against Charles III, Duke of Bourbon, Suzanne's widower, whom she proposed to marry in order to settle the Bourbon inheritance issue. Whe
2 CONC n rejected by Charles, Louise instigated efforts to undermine him. This led to Charles' exile and his attempt to regain his lost status by waging war against the King. He died in 1527 having failed to regain his lost lands and tit
2 CONC les. Louise recovered Auvergne from confiscations and became duchess.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Later life and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy remained active on behalf of her son in the early years of his reign especially. During his absences, she acted as regent on his behalf. She was the principal negotiator for the Treaty of Cambrai between France and
2 CONC  the Holy Roman Empire, concluded on August 3, 1529. That treaty, called "the Ladies' Peace", put an end to the second Italian war between the head of the Valois dynasty, Francis I of France, and the head of the Habsburg dynasty, 
2 CONC Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor. The Treaty temporarily confirmed Habsburg hegemony in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The treaty was signed by Louise of Savoy for France and her sister-in-law, Margaretha von Habsburg (Margaret of Austria), for the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise of Savoy died on 22 September 1531, in Gretz-sur-Loing. The story of her death is that she became chilled while watching a comet. [1] Her remains were entombed at Saint-Denis in Paris. After her death her lands, including A
2 CONC uvergne, merged in the crown. Through her daughter Margaret of Angoulême and her granddaughter Jeanne d'Albret, she is the ancestress of the Bourbon kings of France, as her great-grandson, Henry of Navarre, succeeded as Henry IV o
2 CONC f France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT [show] v • d • eAncestors of Louise of Savoy
