1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_III,_Duke_of_Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles III of Savoy''' (10 October 1486 – 17 August 1553), often called '''Charles the Good''', was Duke of Savoy from 1504 to 1553, although most of his lands were ruled by the French between 1536 and his death. Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT  Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a younger son of Philip (Filippo) the Landless, an aged younger son of the ducal family, and his second wife Claudine de Brosse of the family that unsuccessfully claimed the Duchy of Brittany. His grandparents were Duke Lou
2 CONC is of Savoy and Anne of Cyprus. As a child, there were next to no expectations for him to succeed to any monarchy. He was christened as a namesake of the then-reigning Duke, Charles I of Savoy, the Warrior, his first cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, when he was ten years old, his father unexpectedly succeeded his grandnephew Charles II of Savoy as duke and head of the Savoy dynasty, which had now also received the titles of the kingdoms of Cyprus, Jerusalem and Armen
2 CONC ia. However, Charles's father was not the heir general of the deceased duke, only the male heir. Jerusalem, Cyprus and certain other claims and possessions could go to a different heir, and they did, in principle, going to Charles
2 CONC  II's sister Yolande Louise. Charles's father was not ready to relinquish those, and he took such titles to his own titulary, staking a claim. He also had Yolande marry his son, Philibert the Handsome, in 1496, to ensure the male 
2 CONC line of succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1497, Charles's half-brother Philibert succeeded their father as Duke of Savoy, etc. Philibert however died childless in 1504, surprisingly, and now Charles succeeded, at age eighteen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles faced down challenges to his authority, including from Philibert Berthelier.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT After Yolande's death in 1499, the de jure rights of Jerusalem and Cyprus were lost to the Savoy family. Charles however, as some sort of heir-male, took those titles, which his successors also used. In 1713, Charles's great-great
2 CONC -great-grandson Victor Amadeus II of Savoy received confirmation to that title from the Kings of Spain and France, who also claimed it. The rights, according to succession of heirs general, i.e. not excluding female lines, had gon
2 CONC e, until Charles's death, to the House of La Trémoille, the French lords of La Tremoille, Princes of Talmond and Taranto.
2 CONT
2 CONT In response to the riots between Catholic and Protestants within Geneva, Charles launched a surprise attack in July 1534, but his army was beaten back.[2] A second siege in October 1535 was attempted, and again Charles' army was d
2 CONC efeated when forces from Berne arrived to assist Geneva.[2] Charles was allied with the Habsburg camp in Western European politics, where Francis I of France and Emperor Charles V battled for ascendancy. France invaded Savoy in 15
2 CONC 36, and held almost all of Charles' possessions.[3] He spent the rest of his life practically in exile, at the mercy of relatives. He died in 1553 and was succeeded by his only surviving child, Emanuele Filiberto.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the duke who imprisoned François Bonivard, the "prisoner of Chillon" in 1530. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT -----
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlo II, Duca di Savoia (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #114357, b. 1486, d. 1553
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=9 Mar 2007
2 CONT
2 CONT      Carlo II, Duca di Savoia was born in 1486. (1) He was the son of Filippo II di Bresse, Duca di Savoia. (2) He married Beatriz de Aviz, Infanta de Portugal, daughter of Manuel I de Aviz, Rei de Portugal and Maria de Castilla y
2 CONC  Aragón, Infanta de Castilla, in 1521. (1) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1553. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT      Carlo II, Duca di Savoia also went by the nick-name of Carlo 'the Good' (?). (2) He succeeded to the title of Duca di Savoia in 1504. (1)
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Carlo II, Duca di Savoia and Beatriz de Aviz, Infanta de Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Emanuele Filiberto, Duca di Savoia+3 b. 8 Jul 1528, d. 30 Aug 1580
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p11436.htm#i114357
2 CONT
