1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  The last family member, Yaḵān Begom, was sent to the Safavid court and married to Shah ʿAbbās in 1011/1602 (Falsafi, II, 171-72; Szuppe, 1995, p. 118).  
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT (Shah Abbas I) m. (fifth) at Isfahan, 1st September 1602, Yakshan Begum (b. at Gilan, 1586), daughter of H.E. Khan Ahmad Khan, sometime Governor of Gilan, by his wife, Princess Mariam Sultan Begum [Mirza Begum], daughter of H.M. S
2 CONC ahib-i-Qiran, Sultan bar Salatin, ‘Abu’l Muzaffar ‘Abu’l Fath Sultan Shah Tahmasp bin Shah Ismail al-Safavi al-Husayni al-Musavi Bahadur Khan, Shahanshah of Persia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In 965/1558, Shah Ṭahmāsb decided to install Moẓaffar-Solṭān’s son, Solṭān-Maḥmud, on the throne.  He appointed Khan Aḥmad Khan as his regent.  Later, Khan Aḥmad Khan promised Shah Ṭahmāsb that he would pay a certain tribute of Bi
2 CONC a-pas, let Solṭān-Maḥmud be dethroned, and obtained the province as a fief (eqṭāʿ; Fumani, pp. 33-35).  But his interventions in neighboring provinces went too far; Khan Aḥmad Khan was arrested and imprisoned for 10 years (976-86/
2 CONC 1568-78; Fumani, pp. 45-47; Qāżī Aḥmad Qomi, I, pp. 469-77; Rumlu, pp. 558-66; ʿAbdi Beg,pp. 128-29; Budāq, pp. 226-30).  He was set free by the new shah, Sultan Moḥammad Ḵodābanda (985/1578; Fumani, pp. 64-65; Eskandar Beg, I, pp
2 CONC . 227-28, tr. Savory, I, pp. 219, 339; Mollā Jalāl, p. 43; Naṭanzi, 464-66), who married him to Ṭahmāsb’s daughter, Maryam Begom.  The couple eventually had a daughter, Yaḵān Begom.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
2 CONT https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%DB%8C%D8%A7%DA%A9%D9%87%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A8%DB%8C%DA%AF%D9%85
2 CONT
2 CONT Yakhan Begum (born 1586 in Gilan) was the companion queen of Shah Abbas I.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1590, Yakhan Begum became the candidate for Mohammad Baqir Mirza, the eldest son and crown prince of Shah Abbas I, and this was a political connection until the only heir of Gilan was married to the Safavid crown prince and aft
2 CONC er that Gilan lost its independence. After the escape of Khan Ahmad, he and his mother were sent to the Chehelston Palace in Qazvin. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1602, the issue of Mohammad Baqir Mirza's marriage to Yakhan Begum was raised again, but this marriage did not take place due to the opposition of the prince, so Shah Abbas married him on September 1, 1602. [2]
