1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Vale-Dmitri I, Moskva tsaaririigi troonipretendent alates 1604. aastast ja tsaar 1605–1606
2 CONT * https://et.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vale-Dmitri_I
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''False Dmitry I''' (Cyrillic: Лжедмитрий; other transliterations: Dimitri, Dimitrii, Dimitriy, Dimitry, Dmitri, Dmitrii, Dmitriy) was the Tsar of Russia from 21 July 1605 until his death on 17 May 1606 under the name of Dimitrij
2 CONC  Ioannovich (Cyrillic: Димитрий Иоаннович). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry began talking about himself as the son of Ivan IV for the first time in Hoszcza. In support of his words, he showed a cross that had been gifted to him by his godfather Prince Ivan Mstislavsky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lew Sapieha had a man fleeing from Muscovy named Petrusha Kolobov, who had previously served Tsarevich Dmitry in Uglich. In Poland he was called Petrowski. He was shown to Dmitry without mentioning that Petrusha had once served in
2 CONC  Uglich. Dmitry recognized him immediately. Kolobov also recognized Dmitry as the son of Ivan IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry was fluent in Polish and Latin, was a skilled rider and an European educated person.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grigory Otrepyev spoke only one language - Russian, he could not ride and he had never attended school. His only education was his time at the Chudovo Monastery in Moscow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nuncius Claudio Rangoni described Dmitry as a polite young man with a good physique, who spoke bravely, was in good manners and behaved well.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moscow Patriarch Hiob recruited Grigory Otrepyev, who turned out to be a drunkard and thief with a rude behavior who was soon forced to flee to Poland in 1593 for fear of punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boris Godunov, who declared Dmitry to be Grigory Otrepyev, had never seen Dmitry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dmitry I was not Grigory Otrepyev. We do not know if he was really the son of Ivan Grozny. No one ever knew who he really was. 
