1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_I_de_Bourbon,_prince_de_Cond%C3%A9
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis, Prince of Condé (1530–1569)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Louis I de Bourbon, prince de Condé)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I, Prince of Condé.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis de Bourbon (7 May 1530 – March 13, 1569) was a prominent Huguenot leader and general, the founder of the House of Condé, a cadet branch of the House of Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 1.1 Children
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 Prince de Condé
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Vendôme, he was the fifth son of Charles de Bourbon, Duke of Vendôme, the younger brother of Antoine de Bourbon who married Jeanne d'Albret, Queen of Navarre; their son, Condé's nephew, became Henry IV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a general in the French army, Condé fought at the siege of Metz in 1552, when Francis, Duke of Guise successfully defended the city from the forces of Emperor Charles V, and again at the Battle of St. Quentin in 1557. After his
2 CONC  conversion to Protestantism, he is suspected to have become involved in the Conspiracy of Amboise in 1560, a plot by the Huguenots and members of the House of Bourbon to abduct the adolescent King Francis II and usurp the power o
2 CONC f the House of Guise, who were the leaders of the Catholic party. The plot failed, leading to the massacre of many Huguenots.
2 CONT
2 CONT Condé commanded the Huguenots in the Wars of Religion and was captured at Dreux in 1562. At Orléans, the duke of Guise was assassinated, and when the Queen's fears that the war might drag on led her to negotiate a truce, Condé neg
2 CONC otiated the Peace of Amboise with the Catholic party in 1563, which gave the Huguenots some religious toleration. In another religious civil war, Condé was killed at Jarnac in the Battle of Jarnac, 1569.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Henri, also became a Huguenot general.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1551, Eléanor de Roucy de Roye (1536-1564)[1], heiress of Charles de Roye, who had married Madeleine de Maillé, a half-sister of Coligny.[2] She brought as her dowry the château and small town of Conti-sur-Selles, so
2 CONC uthwest of Amiens, which would pass to their youngest son, progenitor of the princes de Bourbon-Conti. They had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Henri de Bourbon, Prince of Condé
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Marguerite de Bourbon b. 8 Nov 1556
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles de Bourbon b. 3 Nov 1557
2 CONT
2 CONT     * François de Bourbon, Prince of Conti b. 19 Aug 1558
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles de Bourbon, Cardinal, Archbishop of Rouen, b. 30 Mar 1562, Gandelu en Brie
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Bourbon b. 30 Mar 1562
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Madeleine de Bourbon b. 7 Oct 1563
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Catherine de Bourbon b. 1564
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 Nov 1565 he married Francoise d'Orleans, Mademoiselle de Longueville[1], their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles de Bourbon, Count of Soissons b. 3 Nov 1566, Nogent le Rotrou
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis de Bourbon b. 1567
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Benjamin de Bourbon b. 1569
2 CONT
