1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaq I was the son of Yusuf II Jaqeli, pasha of Akhaltsikhe (1680-1690), born of the Georgian feudal family Jaqeli or Djaqeli who continued to rule the Samtskhe on behalf of the Ottoman Empire since its conversion 
2 CONC to Islam in the early seventeenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaq I managed to establish himself as Pasha after a bloody conflict that pitted his uncle Salim I Jaqeli Pasha of 1690-1701, and the latter's son, his cousin Aslan II Jaqeli, Pasha of 1705-1708 and from 1716 to 1718 .
2 CONT
2 CONT A faithful vassal of the Ottomans, Isaq I participated in the conquest of Eastern Georgia (Kartli and Kakheti) by the Ottomans in 1723/1724 . After the death of King Jesse of Kartli Mustafa Pasha in 1727, he became the governor of
2 CONC  the province from 1727 to 1736. It imposes exorbitant taxes and promote the Islamization of the population.
2 CONT
2 CONT The offensive by the Persians under the leadership of the future Nadir Shah hunting eastern Georgia. September 17, 1734, he made the citadel of Tiflis and in 1736 he was replaced by a Bagratid also converted to Islam, Abdullah Beg
2 CONC  Archil II .
2 CONT
2 CONT Removed from power, Isaq Jaqeli I returned to the Samtskhe when he abdicated in favor of his son Yusuf Jaqeli III ( 1737-1744 ) . Opon his death, Isaq Jaqeli became Pasha until his own death in 1750. He was the last hereditary pas
2 CONC ha of Akhaltsikhe Jaqeli the family before the annexation of the province by the Ottoman Empire.
