1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Claude de La Trémoille
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude de La Trémoille, 2nd duke of Thouars, (1566 – 25 October 1604) was a sixteenth-century French nobleman of the La Tremoille family. He was the son of Louis III de La Trémoille and his wife, Jeanne de Montmorency.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry IV who had been friendly with La Trémoille when he was king of Navarre, but kept him in a subordinate position when he became king of France, preferring La Trémoille's cousin, Henry de La Tour d'Auvergne, the Viscount o
2 CONC f Turenne. In 1587, La Trémoille converted to Protestantism. He fought for Henri IV with Coutras and Ivry, and was rewarded by elevation to the peerage, as duke of Thouars, in 1595. This new title, however, made La Trémoille lose 
2 CONC more money than it earned him. In 1598, Turenne proposed to his sister-in-law Charlotte-Brabantine to marry La Trémoille. Thanks to her relations with the houses of Orange and Bouillon, Charlotte-Brabantine played an important par
2 CONC t in the French Protestant diplomacy. They had four children: Henry; Charlotte, who married James Stanley, Earl of Derby; Élisabeth (1601-1604); and Frédéric (1602-1642) comte de Laval.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1602, Charlotte-Brabantine dissuaded her husband from engaging in the conspiracy of Biron and encouraged him to lend allegiance to the king. He died in 1604.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_de_La_Tr%C3%A9moille
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude de La Trémoille, 2nd duke of Thouars (1566 – 25 October 1604) was a sixteenth-century French nobleman of the La Tremoille family. He was the son of Louis III de La Trémoille and his wife, Jeanne de Montmorency.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Henry IV who had been friendly with La Trémoille when he was king of Navarre, but kept him in a subordinate position when he became king of France, preferring La Trémoille's cousin, Henry de La Tour d'Auvergne, the Viscount o
2 CONC f Turenne. In 1587, La Trémoille converted to Protestantism. He fought for Henri IV with Coutras and Ivry, and was rewarded by elevation to the peerage, as duke of Thouars, in 1595. This new title, however, made La Trémoille lose 
2 CONC more money than it earned him. In 1598, Turenne proposed to his sister-in-law Charlotte-Brabantine to marry La Trémoille. Thanks to her relations with the houses of Orange and Bouillon, Charlotte-Brabantine played an important par
2 CONC t in the French Protestant diplomacy. They had four children: Henry; Charlotte, who married James Stanley, Earl of Derby; Élisabeth (1601–1604); and Frédéric (1602–1642) comte de Laval.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1602, Charlotte-Brabantine dissuaded her husband from engaging in the conspiracy of Biron and encouraged him to lend allegiance to the king. He died in 1604.
