1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_d%27Angoul%C3%AAme
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/247925270/henri-d&#39;angouleme
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Henri de Valois was born the bastard son of Henri II of France and his mistress Janet Stewart, who was an illegitimate daughter of James IV of Scotland and served as governess to the young Mary, Queen of Scots.[1] Being the most h
2 CONC ighly favored natural son of the King, Henri was legitimized and made Duke of Angoulême. He would later serve as Abbot of La Chaise-Dieu, Grand Prior of France[2] and Admiral of the Levantine Sea, further ruling as Governor of Pro
2 CONC vence from 1579 until his death in 1586.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1570, the supporters of Mary, Queen of Scots, who was exiled in England, selected Henri as a potential leader of a French military force to aid them in their civil war against the supporters James VI of Scotland. They thought t
2 CONC hat Henri's Scottish and French royal ancestry would gain him respect in Scotland and England. Although the Queen's supporters put the idea to John Lesley, Queen Mary's ambassador in France, French soldiers and Henri were not sent
2 CONC  to Scotland.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Tapestry detailing the Siege of La Rochelle (1572-1573)
2 CONT Henri d'Angoulême took a major role in the two extended military battle against Huguenot strongholds during the height of the French Wars of Religion, engaging in the massive Siege of La Rochelle (1572–1573), organized by the Duke
2 CONC  of Anjou, future Henry III of France, and leading the five-year Siege of Ménerbes (1573–1578), fought at a citadel in the Luberon foothills cherished by Pope Pius V.
2 CONT
2 CONT While serving as Governor of Provence, his secretary was the poet François de Malherbe.[4] Henri wrote many sonnets, one of which was set to music by Fabrice Caietain.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1586, Henri was killed at Aix-en-Provence in a duel with Philip Altoviti, who also was mortally wounded in the fight.[5]
2 CONT
