1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GENEALOGIA BETANCOURT
2 CONT
2 CONT Familia canaria, procedente de los Béthencourt de Normandía (Francia), varios de cuyos miembros pasaron a Venezuela. (Nota 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT  I. Mathieu o Mateo de Béthencourt, conocido por Maciot, lugarteniente del Barón normando y primer Señor de las Canarias Jean IV de Béthencourt, en cuyo nombre traspasó los derechos sobre las islas en 1418 al conde de Niebla Enriq
2 CONC ue de Guzmán, que le dejó también el encargo de su gobierno, con los títulos de Capitán y Gobernador Mayor de sus dominios insulares. Fue Señor de Lanzarote hasta 1448, en que traspasó el señorío al Infante Enrique de Portugal, y 
2 CONC se trasladó a vivir a la Madera. Don Maciot y la princesa guanche Teguise, hija de Guadarfia, rey de la isla de Lanzarote, tuvieron por hija a: 
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Da. Inés Margarita de Béthencourt, que testó el 4 de junio de 1480 en Lanzarote ante el escribano Marcos Luzardo. Casó con Arriete Perdomo, caballero francés, quien dio su nombre a un lugar de la isla de Lanzarote aún conocido
2 CONC  por Arrieta, sin duda donde levantó su casa. Tuvieron por hija, entre otros a: 
2 CONT
2 CONT III. Da. Margarita de Béthencourt casó en la isla de Lanzarote con D. Juan Pérez de Munguía, conocido por Perucho de Bilbao, caballero vizcaíno que llegó a  las islas con Diego de Herrera y doña Inés Peraza, Señores de las Canaria
2 CONC s. Don Juan Pérez de Munguía, estando en Sevilla, otorgó el 29 de abril de 1489, ante el escribano Juan Ruiz de Porras, contrato con Alfonso Sánchez Peraile para acudir a la conquista de Baza. Tuvieron por hija, entre otros: 1. Da
2 CONC . María de Béthencourt, que sigue. 2. Da. Elvira Pérez de Munguía y Béthencourt casó con D. Luis de León, el Viejo, natural de la ciudad de León, Gobernador de Lanzarote y Fuerteventura, y tuvieron por hijas a: A. María de Bilbao,
2 CONC  fallecida para abril de 1547, casó con su primo hermano D. Marcos Verde de Béthencourt, que veremos. Iseo de León tuvo con Pedro Fernández de Saavedra y Escobar, el Mozo, hijo de Fernán Arias de Saavedra y Sarmiento de Herrera y 
2 CONC Catalina Escobar de las Roelas a: D. Diego Sarmiento de Ayala Rojas y Sandoval, nacido en Lanzarote hacia 1535 y fallecido en Teguise en 1607, fue Alguacil Mayor del Santo Oficio de La información de las primeras catorce generacio
2 CONC nes que se estudian en el Nobiliario de Canarias, Tomo III, págs. 595 a 609, y 627 a 647. Existe también un árbol genealógico elaborado por D. Eduardo Ginés en poder de la familia Pérez Recao. Regidor de la Inquisición de Canarias
2 CONC  desde julio de 1582. Casó hacia 1562, previa dispensa del cuarto grado de consanguinidad, con doña María de Ayala y Peraza, nacida en Sevilla hacia 1540, Señora de las Salinas de Lanzarote, fallecida en Gran Canaria en 1598, hija
2 CONC  de Hernán Peraza Dumpierres, Alguacil Mayor del Santo Oficio de la Inquisición, y de María de Ayala y Montemayor (Véase HERRERA, Varonía de Saavedra). 
2 CONT
2 CONT IV. Da. María de Béthencourt, quien, viuda, otorgó escritura el 20 de mayo de 1524 en La Laguna, Tenerife, ante el escribano Alonso de Llerena. Casó con D. Juan Verde, natural de Córdoba (Andalucía), a quien la tradición genealógi
2 CONC ca hace caballero de Santiago. Fueron vecinos de la isla de Lanzarote. (...).
2 CONT Fuentes bibliográficas (Notas):
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) La información de las primeras catorce generaciones se estudian en el Nobiliario de Canarias, Tomo III, págs. 595 a 609, y 627 a 647. 
2 CONT ___________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''BÉTTENCOURT, Matthieu [Maciot, Maciet] I de'''<br/>
2 CONT Su nombre fue castellanizado como mosén Masiote.<br/>
2 CONT Sobrino de Jean de Béttencourt, del cual, en sus ausencias, detentaba el gobierno del Señorío insular. Cfr. ''Matritense,'' caps. 1 y 2.<br/>
2 CONT Sobrino de Jean de Béttencourt, del cual, en sus ausencias, tenía el gobierno del Señorío insular. Vendió este Señorío al infante don Henrique, según unas fuentes, y a *Pedro Barba de Campos, según otras. Cfr. Frutuoso, ''op. cit.
2 CONC '', libro l.<br/>
2 CONT Recibió en herencia de su pariente Jean la conquista y el Señorío de las Islas. Él lo vendió al sevillano Pedro Barba de Campos. Cfr. Espinosa, ''op. cit.'', libro III, cap. 2.<br/>
2 CONT Castellanizado como Masiote o Masión de Betancor. Le da el tratamiento de primo y sobrino de Jean. Vino a la conquista y repoblación de las Islas, tomando asiento en Lanzarote. Quedó con el gobierno de las Islas a la marcha de *Je
2 CONC an de Béttencourt y fue su heredero a su muerte. Se concierta con Pedro Barba, comisionado del Rey, del conde de Niebla y de Guillén de las Casas, quedando el señorío de las Islas en el conde de Niebla (año 1418). Defraudó el trat
2 CONC o, sin embargo, ya que hizo lo mismo con el infante don Henrique y pasó a vivir a Madeira. Cfr. ''Abreu, op. cit.,'' libro l, caps. 8, 19, 20, 21, 22 y 25; y libro III, cap. 6.<br/>
2 CONT ''El hacer tradicional se inventó a la princesa lanzaroteña Teguise a partir de un error del ''Abreu''. Éste, al hablar de la familia que formaron Maciot II y ''Tenaguana'', lo hizo con una redacción confusa. De ello, queda la dud
2 CONC a de si llama Luisa de Betancor a la ''hija del rey de Lanzarote'' o a la mujer de Juan de Arriete.''<br/>
2 CONT ''La investigación documental parece indicar otro camino. Luisa de Betancor fue grancanaria, sobrina de los guadartemes de Telde y Gáldar, c. con Maciot II; en Teguise , mientras que la mujer de Arriete Perdomo pudo llamarse Marga
2 CONC rita o Inés de Betancor y ser entenada [hijastra] de Maciot I.''<br/>
2 CONT Sucedió a su tío Juan de Letancur en el Señorío y conquista de las Islas. Las vendió al sevillano Pedro Barba. Viana, ''Poema ...,'' Canto II.<br/>
2 CONT Pariente lejano de Jean, hijo de Jean d'Argies, señor de Béttencourt y de Marie de Braquemont. Cfr. Bergeron, ''Le Canarien,'' París, 1630.<br/>
2 CONT '''Debió venir a las Islas, en la década de 1410, con *Jean de Béttencourt. El parentesco que se le da documentalmente es el de primo. Tal vez, por la línea de los Braquemont, pudo llamársele sobrino (derivado de ser hijo de primo
2 CONC  hermano o primo).'''<br/>
2 CONT Junto con otros parientes recibió poderes de Jean para gobernar y explotar las Islas. Terminó por quedar él como apoderado y gobernador único. En 1418 vendió el derecho señorial al conde de Niebla y él permaneció con la tenientía 
2 CONC insular. En 1430, favorecido por la transaccción entre el conde de Niebla y los Casaus , mantuvo la gobernación y señorío condicionado de la isla de Lanzarote, que perdió al pactar con el infante don Henrique de Viseu. '''Se asent
2 CONC ó en Madeira,''' donde había recibido una generosa donación del Infante. Quedó desvinculado totalmente de las Islas en 1454.<br/>
2 CONT '''Casó con una Marie, con la que tuvo a su única hija, llamada María de Betancor''' c., en Madeira, con Ruy Gonzales Zarco, y que no tuvo sucesión. Viudo , hemos supuesto que se casó en Lanzarote con Margarite, viuda a su vez de 
2 CONC *Henri de Béttencourt, matrimonio que lo convirtió en padrastro de Margarita , la mujer de Arriete II Perdomo (CEBRIÁN, José A.: ''Ensayo para un diccionario de conquistadores de Canarias.'' Islas Canarias: Viceconsejería de Cultu
2 CONC ra y Deportes del Gobierno de Canarias 2003. pp. 123-124).
2 CONT
2 CONT OLA
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Maciot de Béthencourt''' born (illegitimate) about 1390 at France, died 1454 at  Madeira, Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Lord of the Canary Islands, Barón de Saint Martín le Gaillard, knight of the Order of Rhodes, knight of the Order of Malta
2 CONT
2 CONT There are different views on how he is related to the Bettencourts of France. There is a theory that Maciot was not the son of Regnault but of Regnault's sister of unknown name. Another theory states that he is the son of Jean (II
2 CONC I)'s sister of unknown name. Current opinion though do favor Regnault as his parent.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT As to Maciot's wife and children, there are even more problems. Maciot married Lerianda de Gardaleme at France.  However, Lerianda de Gardaleme is possibly confused with the wife of Maciot's grandson, Maciot (II). Her name is very
2 CONC  similar - Leissa Guanartème, her name after converting to Catholicism. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT The ''Ascendência e Descendência do Conselheiro Nicolau Anastácio de Bettencourt'' gives Marguérite de Béthencourt's father as Jean IV instead of Maciot. It also states that Jean IV married a second time, in 1415, to Lerize Guarda
2 CONC teme, Princess of the Royal House of the Canarias and Lady of the Island of Lanzarote, Marguérite's mother. In a note, however, it states that various authors give Marguérite's father as Maciot. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT On 9 May 1405 Maciot traveled to the Canary Islands with his Uncle Jean.  He was ruler of the Canary Islands after his uncle left the islands between Dec 1405 and 1447. Maciot attempted, with the assistance of some Castilians, to 
2 CONC subdue the island of Gomeira, Islas Canarias, but without success. In 1414 the exactions and tyranny of Maciot de Bethencourt had caused Queen Catherine of Castile to send out three war caravels under the command of Pedro Barba de
2 CONC  Campos, Lord of Castro Forte, to control him. Maciot, although only regent, since Jean de Bethencourt was still alive, ceded the islands to Barba and then sailed to Madeira, where he sold to Prince Henry of Portugal, surnamed the
2 CONC  Navigator, these very islands which he had just ceded to another, together with those which still remained to be conquered. (According to Os Bettencourt, this sale by Maciot to Campos is pure legend) Maciot subsequently granted t
2 CONC hem again to the Spanish Count de Niebla on 15 Nov 1419 (1418 according to Os Bettencourt). Pedro Barba de Campos sold them to Fernando Perez of Seville, and the latter again to the aforesaid Count de Niebla, who disposed of them 
2 CONC to Guillem de Las Casas, and the latter to his son-in-law Fernan Peraza. Meanwhile, the legitimate proprietor, Jean de Bethencourt, left them by will to his brother Reynaud. It was not until 1479, with the treaty signed at Alcaçov
2 CONC a between Affonso V of Portugal and Ferdinand and Isabella of Castile, that the disputes of the two nations on this point were settled - the Canaries belonged to the Castilians.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1415 in Lanzarote, Maciot had a relationship with Teguise, a native woman and daughter of King Guardafia. With regard to Maciot's relationship with Teguise, it seems that they were never married. They had at least two children,
2 CONC  Rodrigo and Inês Margarida. Portuguese genealogists affirm that Maria, another of Maciot's daughters, was also a daughter of Teguise; in fact, Canarian genealogists refer to this Maria as Leonor or Luisa.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT He built the church of Nossa Senhora de Bethencourt; acting as representative of Jean de Bethencourt, granted the lordship of the Canary Islands to D. Henrique de Guzmán, Conde de Niebla on 15 Nov 1418 at Seville.  In 1447 Maciot 
2 CONC was taken prisoner on Lanzarote and imprisoned on the island of Ferro. He later fled with his wife to Portugal. He sold Lanzarote to Prince Henry the Navigator for the soap trade and other income from Madeira on 9 May 1448. He mov
2 CONC ed with his two daughters and two of his brother's sons to Madeira (Os Bettencourt states that he went with his wife, his eldest daughter who was the widow of Henri (II), and her two sons who were also his brother's two sons since
2 CONC  his daughter was married to his brother (niece-uncle). It was the descendants of Henri (II) who altered the "th" of Bethencourt to the "tt" of Bettencourt). He resided at Ribeira Brava. He died between 1454 and 1458 at Madeira.<b
2 CONC r/>
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Nacido en Francia, marchó como lugarteniente de su tío a las islas Canarias, y allí, según Rodrigo Rodrigues, casó con Teguine (o Lérida) Guarda Teme (¿Guanarteme?), probablemente -en mi opinión y por el apellido- una princesa gua
2 CONC nche. Trasladado Maciot a Madeira hacia 1450, llevó consigo a la isla portuguesa, entre otros, a dos de sus hijas y allí habrían de venir al mundo sus nietos. Al parecer, su esposa canaria había fallecido en Lanzarote.
2 CONT
2 CONT María del Carmen Vaquero, "Doña Beatriz de Sá, la Elisa posible de Garcilaso". Revista Lemir, nº 7, 2003 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Maciot de8 BETHENCOURT (Regnault7, Jean6, Jean5, Jean4, Regnault3, Philippe2, Jean1)672,673,674,675,676,677,678,679,680,681 was born illegitimate circa 1390 in France.682,683,684 He married Lerianda de Gardaleme in France.685,686,
2 CONC 687 He died between 1454 and 1458 in Madeira.688
2 CONT
2 CONT He was also known as Mossem Menante de Betancor.689 He was also known as Maciot de Bethancourt.690 He was also known as Maciot de Bettencourt.691,692 He was also known as Micer Maciote Betancur.693 He was also known as Massiote de
2 CONC  Bethencourt.694 He was also known as Masiote de Bethencourt.695 He was also known as Mathieu de Bethencourt.696,697 He was also known as Menciot de Bettencourt.698 He was also known as Mossem Maciot de Betancor.699 He was also kn
2 CONC own as Meciot de Bettencourt.700 He was also known as Meciote de Bettencourt.701 Maciot is a problem in many ways. There are different views on how he is related to the Bettencourts of France. There is a theory that Maciot was not
2 CONC  the son of Regnault but of Regnault's sister of unknown name. Another theory states that he is the son of Jean (III)'s sister of unknown name. Current opinion favors Regnault as his parent.
2 CONT
2 CONT As to Maciot's wife and children, there are even more problems. Lerianda de Gardaleme is possibly confused with the wife of Maciot's grandson, Maciot (II). Her name is very similar - Leissa Guanartème, her name after converting to
2 CONC  Catholicism.702 He was a knight of the Order of Rhodes.703 He was a knight of the Order of Malta.704,705,706 On 9 May 1405 Maciot traveled to the Canary Islands with his Uncle Jean.707,708,709 He was ruler of the Canary Islands a
2 CONC fter his uncle left the islands between Dec 1405 and 1447.710,711,712,713,714,715,716,717 Maciot attempted, with the assistance of some Castilians, to subdue the island of Gomeira, Islas Canarias, but without success.718 In 1414 t
2 CONC he exactions and tyranny of Maciot de Bethencourt had caused Queen Catherine of Castile to send out three war caravels under the command of Pedro Barba de Campos, Lord of Castro Forte, to control him. Maciot, although only regent,
2 CONC  since Jean de Bethencourt was still alive, ceded the islands to Barba and then sailed to Madeira, where he sold to Prince Henry of Portugal, surnamed the Navigator, these very islands which he had just ceded to another, together 
2 CONC with those which still remained to be conquered. (According to Os Bettencourt, this sale by Maciot to Campos is pure legend) Maciot subsequently granted them again to the Spanish Count de Niebla on 15 Nov 1419 (1418 according to O
2 CONC s Bettencourt). Pedro Barba de Campos sold them to Fernando Perez of Seville, and the latter again to the aforesaid Count de Niebla, who disposed of them to Guillem de Las Casas, and the latter to his son-in-law Fernan Peraza. Mea
2 CONC nwhile, the legitimate proprietor, Jean de Bethencourt, left them by will to his brother Reynaud. It was not until 1479, with the treaty signed at Alcaçova between Affonso V of Portugal and Ferdinand and Isabella of Castile, that 
2 CONC the disputes of the two nations on this point were settled - the Canaries belonged to the Castilians.719,720 In 1415 in Lanzarote, Maciot had a relationship with Teguise, a native woman and daughter of King Guardafia.721,722,723 W
2 CONC ith regard to Maciot's relationship with Teguise, it seems that they were never married. They had at least two children, Rodrigo and Inês Margarida. Portuguese genealogists affirm that María, another of Maciot's daughters, was als
2 CONC o a daughter of Teguise; in fact, Canarian genealogists refer to this María as Leonor or Luisa.724 He built the church of Nossa Senhora de Bethencourt; .725 He, acting as representative of Jean de Bethencourt, granted the lordship
2 CONC  of the Canary Islands to D. Henrique de Guzmán, Conde de Niebla on 15 Nov 1418 in Seville.726,727,728 In 1447 Maciot was taken prisoner on Lanzarote and imprisoned on the island of Ferro. He later fled with his wife to Portugal.7
2 CONC 29 He sold Lanzarote to Prince Henry the Navigator for the soap trade and other income from Madeira on 9 May 1448.730,731,732,733,734,735,736,737,738 He moved with his two daughters and two of his brother's sons to Madeira (Os Bet
2 CONC tencourt states that he went with his wife, his eldest daughter who was the widow of Henri (II), and her two sons who were also his brother's two sons since his daughter was married to his brother (niece-uncle). It was the descend
2 CONC ants of Henri (II) who altered the "th" of Bethencourt to the "tt" of Bettencourt).739,740,741,742,743,744,745
2 CONT
2 CONT Lerianda de GARDALEME746 was born circa 1390 in France.747 She married Henri de Bettencourt, son of Regnault de Bethencourt (IV) and Felipa de Troyes, in France (possible spouse).748 She was also known as Lerija de Guardateme.749 
2 CONC She was also known as Lerida de Guardateme.750 She was also known as Lerida de La Garde Tour.751 She was also known as Lerida de Guarde Teme.752 She was also known as Lerida de Guardatem.753 She was also known as Lerisa de Guadate
2 CONC me.754 She is possibly confused with the wife of Maciot's grandson, Maciot (II).755
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Maciot de8 Bethencourt and Lerianda de Gardaleme were as follows:
