2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Royal titles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Royal titles in Latin: Vladislaus Quartus Dei gratia rex Poloniae, magnus dux Lithuaniae, Russiae, Prussiae, Masoviae, Samogitiae, Livoniaeque, necnon Suecorum, Gothorum Vandalorumque haereditarius rex, electus magnus dux Mo
2 CONC schoviae.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * English translation: Vladislaus IV by God's grace king of Poland, grand duke of Lithuania, Ruthenia, Prussia, Masovia, Samogitia, Livonia, and also hereditary king of the Swedes, Goths and Vandals, elected Grand Duke of Mosc
2 CONC ow.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Władysław Zygmunt Waza-Jagiellon, in 1632 he was elected King of Poland. By paternal inheritance, he legally succeeded as King of Sweden. He was also heir to one of the several Christian claims to the title of King of Jerusalem
2 CONC , but the Kingdom of Jerusalem had been defunct for several centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław in young age
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław in young age
2 CONT
2 CONT His father Sigismund III Vasa, grandson of Gustav I of Sweden, had succeeded his father to the Swedish throne in 1592, only to be deposed in 1599 by his uncle, subsequently Charles IX. This led to a long-standing feud where the Po
2 CONC lish kings of the house of Vasa claimed the Swedish throne. The effects of this were the Swedish War (1600-1629) and later, The Deluge of 1655. Sigismund, a devout Catholic, pursued other military conflicts abroad, barely avoiding
2 CONC  involving the Commonwealth in the Thirty Year War and supported counter-reformation, both policies which lead to increasing tensions inside the Commonwealth.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Prince
2 CONT
2 CONT Skarga's Sermon, by Jan Matejko, 1862, oil on canvas, 224 x 397 cm, Royal Castle, Warsaw. Piotr Skarga (standing at right) preaches. King Sigismund III Vasa is seated in the first row, left of center. Just above and to left of Kin
2 CONC g Zygmunt is his son, the future King Władysław IV Vasa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Skarga's Sermon, by Jan Matejko, 1862, oil on canvas, 224 x 397 cm, Royal Castle, Warsaw. Piotr Skarga (standing at right) preaches. King Sigismund III Vasa is seated in the first row, left of center. Just above and to left of Kin
2 CONC g Zygmunt is his son, the future King Władysław IV Vasa.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1610 Władysław, aged 15, was elected Tsar by Russia's aristocracy council of Seven boyars, who overthrew tsar Vasily Shuysky during that country's Time of Troubles. His election was part of an unsuccessful plan by Zygmunt to co
2 CONC nquer all of Russia and convert its population to Catholicism. Władysław was never able to reign in Russia, however, as his support there was temporary and depended on shifting internal politics among the boyars. He held on to the
2 CONC  title, without any real power, until 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before he was elected king of the Commonwealth, he fought in many campaigns, including ones against Russians in 1617-1618 (the end of Dymitriads), Ottomans in 1621 (Battle of Chocim at the end of Moldavian Magnate Wars) and Swedes
2 CONC  in 1626-1629. During that time, as well as during his voyage in Europe (1624-1625) with Albrycht Stanisław Radziwiłł and others, he learned the art of war, and this was later to be reflected when he became king: military matters 
2 CONC were always important to him. While not a military genius, and surpassed by famous Commonwealth contemporary hetmans like Stanisław Koniecpolski, Wladislaw was known as a fairly skilful commander on his own.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] King
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław IV. Drawing by Jan Matejko.
2 CONT
2 CONT Władysław IV. Drawing by Jan Matejko.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Politics
2 CONT
2 CONT   Swedish Royalty
2 CONT
2 CONT   House of Vasa
2 CONT
2 CONT Gustav I
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents
2 CONT
2 CONT    Erik Johansson, Cecilia Månsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    Eric XIV, John III, Catherine, Cecilia, Magnus, Anna Maria, Sophia, Elizabeth, Charles IX
2 CONT
2 CONT Eric XIV
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    Sigrid, Gustav
2 CONT
2 CONT John III
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    Sigismund, Anna, John
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigismund
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    Władysław IV, John II Casimir, John Albert, Charles Ferdinand, Alexander Charles, Anna Catherine Constance
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IX
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    Catherine, Gustav II Adolf, Maria Elizabeth, Christina, Charles Philip
