1 CONC esse d'Étampes, "the most beautiful of learned ladies, and the most learned of beautiful ladies," replaced Madame de Châteaubriant, more as a companion than mistress.   Their raging hatred impelled Charles and Francis to challenge
1 CONC  each other to a duel, which was, however, prevented. During one of the King's relapses, his mother reached an agreement with Margaret of Austria, the Emperor's aunt, to stop this deadly struggle. The ensuing Treaty of Cambrai sof
1 CONC tened that of Madrid. In order to get his children back, Francis had to abandon his allies, give up Italy, and pay 2,000,000 gold crowns. His foolish expenditures had emptied the treasury, and the ransom was collected only with di
