1 CONC f Bethancourt and of Granville."125 He was also known as Jehan de Béthencourt (III) suffix added for clarification.126 After 1350 after Jeanne's marriage to Pierre de Neuville, her brother Jean (III) granted to her an annual incom
1 CONC e of 50 libras from property situated in Béthencourt, a grant which he confirmed in 1362, the date of her second marriage.127 He was Lord chamberlain of the Duke of Burgundy.128 One source, in obvious error, states that in 1430 at
1 CONC  the side of the Burgundians, allied with the English, he was in the siege of Compiègne (in which Joan of Arc was captured), having also been imprisoned, by the French, and subsequently ransomed.129
1 CONT
